<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolesław III Wrymouth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1086">1086</a>–<a href="/years/1138">1138</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolesław III Wrymouth was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1102 to 1138. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, defended Poland against the German emperor Henry V in 1109 and promoted the Christianisation of the Pomeranians. His testament of 1138 divided the realm among his sons, with the senior duke ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, beginning almost two centuries of fragmentation into duchies such as Greater Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth.jpg" alt="Bolesław III Wrymouth" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Societas Scientiarum Plocensis (Towarzystwo Naukowe Płockie), based on the sketches of Jerzy Mazuś. · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boleslaw_III_Wrymouth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Polish rulers had tried to subdue them since the tenth century, and around 1121–1122 Duke <b>Bolesław III Wrymouth</b> of Poland conquered the lands around the lower Oder.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the testament of <b>Boleslaw III</b> in 1138, his son Mieszko III the Old received Greater Poland, with Poznan and Gniezno, the cradle of the Piast state.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>In his testament of 1138 <b>Boleslaw III Wrymouth</b> divided Poland among his sons, creating a senior province around Krakow to be held by the eldest Piast with authority over the others.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krakow">Duchy of Krakow</a></li><li><blockquote>Masovia, on the middle Vistula with its centre at Plock, was given to Boleslaw IV the Curly under the testament of <b>Boleslaw III</b> in 1138.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-masovia">Duchy of Masovia</a></li><li><blockquote>Eastern Pomerania, around Gdansk on the Vistula delta, was conquered by <b>Boleslaw III</b> of Poland in the early 12th century and governed for him by local nobles.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerelia">Duchy of Pomerelia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Boleslaw III</b>'s testament of 1138 assigned Sandomierz and its land to his young son Henry, who took control in 1146.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-sandomierz">Duchy of Sandomierz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in Sochaczew, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1119" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5007 422 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bolesław III Wrymouth"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5457 692h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1119">What was here in 1119? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
